# Untitled

Updated Metal Compounds (MOFs, \(-S\), \(-OH\), \(-N\), \(-C\)) Used as Cathode Materials for Lithium–Sulfur Batteries
Lithium–sulfur (Li–S) batteries have the potential to be as efficient and as widespread as lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries, since sulfur electrode has high theoretical capacity (1672 mA h \(\mathbf{g}_{\mathbf{su}}^{-1}\)) and this element is affordable. However, unlike their ubiquitous lithium ion (Li-ion) counterparts, it is difficult to realize the commercialization of Li-S battery. Because the shuttle effect of polysulfide inevitably results in the serious capacity degradation. Tremendous progress is devoted to approach this problem from the aspect of physical confinement and chemisorption of polysulfide. Owing to weak intermolecular interactions, physical confinement strategy, however is not effective when the battery is cycled long-term. Chemisorption of polysulfide that derived from polar–polar interaction, Lewis acid–base interaction, and sulfur-chain catenation, are proven to significantly suppress the shuttle effect of polysulfide. It is also discovered that the metal compounds have strong chemical interactions with polysulfide. Therefore, this review focuses on latest metal–organic frameworks metal sulfides, metal hydroxides, metal nitrides, metal carbides, and discusses how the chemical interactions couple with the unique properties of these metal compounds to tackle the problem of polysulfide shuttle effect.
## 1. Introduction
The energy storage market has experienced a boom in its technical development and growth over the last few decades, this can be attributed to its wide usefulness in a range of applications including smart power grids, electric vehicles and portable electronic devices. It has been reported that demand is expected to increase from 27 GWh in 2017 to 90 GWh within three years, at an annual growth rate of over \(20\%\).[1] An important aspect of this demand is the pressure that will
place on energy storage systems to contain higher energy density than is currently available. This demand, however will eventually meet the density limits of conventional Li-ion battery systems sometime within the next 10 years. Out of the current battery systems available, Li–S batteries have high theoretical energy density of 2567 Wh \(\mathrm{kg}_{\mathrm{su}}^{-1}\), this is five times higher than the currently available metal oxide cathode and graphite anode based Li-ion battery (Figure 1a). Since the practical energy density of Li–S battery in the future has been estimated to be 500–600 Wh \(\mathrm{kg}_{\mathrm{su}}^{-1}\), which is more than twice that of a state-of-the-art Li-ion battery, there is great interest in developing practical examples of these types of batteries, especially in such applications as electric cars where customers are demanding a \(500\;\;\mathrm{km}\) range from a single charge.[2] Coupling with its cost-effectiveness and natural abundance, these attribute Li–S batteries a promising energy storage system for large-scale
applications compared with other rechargeable battery systems, and this is illustrated in Figure 1b.[3]
In contrast to the conventional Li-ion battery, Li–S battery uses a different intercalation process that involves a multiple-electron transfer reaction \((\mathrm{S}_{8}+16\ \mathrm{Li}^{+}+16\ \mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow8\ \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})\), which have several reactions happen like \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\rightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}\rightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\rightarrow\) \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\).[3,5] As can be seen in Figure 1c,[4] the long chain polysulfides can be easily dissolved in their organic electrolyte while lithium sulfides \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2},\ \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})\) cannot. The main obstacles impeding the commercialization of the Li–S batteries are: (1) the insulating nature of the sulfur and lithium sulfides; (2) the dissolution properties of the intermediate polysulfides \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{x}\) \((4\leq x\leq8)\)) in the electrolyte; (3) the large volumetric expansion \((80\%)\) of the sulfur upon its lithiation; and (4) the limitations associated with using a metallic lithium anode.[5]
Enormous efforts have been devoted to the development of cathode materials for high performance Li–S batteries, and a considerable amount of that focus on the encapsulation of sulfur particles with various carbon matrix materials, such as carbon nanofibers, carbon spheres, graphene sheets, as these carbon-based materials have good compatibility with sulfur. However, this rather weak intermolecular interaction between the nonpolar hydrophobic carbonaceous materials and the more polar hydrophobic lithium polysulfide materials is not sufficient to prevent diffusion and shuttling of polysulfide, which
always result in serious capacity degradation for the long-term cycling.[6] While the chemical interactions derived from polar materials, including polar–polar interaction, Lewis acid–base interaction, and sulfur-chain catenation, are proved to significantly suppress the shuttle effect of polysulfide. Nazar's group[7] comprehensively summarized the binding energy metrics for chemical interactions between lithium polysulfides and different sulfur hosts. Mai and co-workers[8] widely summarized recent developments in the use of nanostructured metal oxides or their composites as cathode host materials for Li–S batteries. However, classified reviews about the chemical interactions derived from various metal compounds \((-S, -N, -OH, -C, MOFs)\) for polysulfide absorption have been rarely reported. Herein, for the first time, latest advances about popular materials such as metal organic frameworks, metal sulfides, metal hydroxides, metal nitrides, as well as MXene were reviewed and how the chemical interactions couple with the unique properties of these metal compounds to tackle the problem of polysulfide shuttle effect were comprehensively discussed, and a perspective on efforts to produce more reliable Li–S batteries through a rational approach was also provided.
2. The Starting States of Sulfur (Elemental Sulfur, Short-Chain Sulfur, Lithium Sulfide, and Lithium Polysulfides) for Li–S Batteries
With respect to the cathodes used in Li–S batteries, sulfurstarting states can be categorized in terms of whether it contains elemental sulfur \((S_{8})\), small sulfur \((S_{2-4})\), lithium sulfide \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})\) or lithium polysulfides \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{8}, 4 \leq x \leq 8)\). There were various reports about different types of cathodes that use elemental sulfur as the starting material, such as carbon materials,[9] metal oxides,[10] metal sulfides,[11] metal nitrides,[12] metal–organic frameworks (MOFs),[13] MXene,[14] and carbon nitrides[17] based cathodes, and they are shown in Figure 2a. With most of these host materials, sulfur exists in the form of cyclo- \(\cdot S_{8}\) molecule with \(0.7\;\mathrm{nm}^{[15]}\) in at least two dimensions and exhibits the multistep reduction sequence of \(\mathrm{S}_{8} \rightarrow \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8} \rightarrow \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4} \rightarrow \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) with two typical plateaus, this corresponds to the transformation of cyclo- \(\cdot S_{8}\) to high-order lithium polysulfides \((2.4\;\mathrm{~V~})\) or high-order lithium polysulfides to lithium sulfides \((2.1\;\mathrm{~V~})\), respectively. During this process the theoretical capacity generated was reported to be about \(1672\;\mathrm{mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{sal}}{}^{-1}\), so this type of sulfur cathode possesses the advantages of a relatively high discharge voltage plateau as well as the potential for high sulfur content in the cathode.[17,19] However, this system can still have polysulfide dissolution and electromechanical damage caused by the large volume expansion of the sulfur as it undergoes lithiation. Apart from these concerns, metallic lithium anode can also give rise to dendrite formation as well as the possibility of security problem.[4,17]
Small sulfur molecules refer to molecules with short-chain sulfur \((S_{2-4})\) that are distributed in a conductive matrix at the molecular scale and directly transition from \(S_{2-4}\) to the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}S\) phase while avoiding the unfavorable transition from \(S_{8}\) to \(S_{4}{}^{2-}\) phase, examples of these are shown in Figure 2b. A short chain sulfur is compatible with a carbonate electrolyte, so no specially
