was first prepared by pyrolysis of D-glucose that had a pore size of \(0.5\;\mathrm{nm}\), and was then used to trap the metastable small sulfur molecules, which has theoretical sulfur loading of about \(50\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) in the composite. In another report, Wang et al. applied elemental sulfur to a dehydrogenate polyacrylonitrile (PAN)[21] polar side chain \(-\mathrm{CN}\) groups in a PAN cyclized to form a heterocyclic compound where sulfur was embedded and incorporated. This resulted in the formation of a functional PAN, but it should be noted that the small sulfur-based cathodes always have a sulfur content that less than \(50\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) of the composites, and the discharge voltage plateau \((1.9\;\mathrm{eV})\) is relatively low, which meant that its energy density is also low.
Recently, a lithium sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)\) cathode with a theoretical capacity of \(1166\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{Li}_{12S}{}^{-1}\) has attracted attention because of its compatibility with the much safer Li-free anodes such as those made out of graphite or Si/Sn-based composites, and this cathode type is shown in Figure 3a.[22–25] Since lithium has already incorporated into the structure of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), the volume expansion of sulfur can be effectively avoided, making it much
easier to form a stable electrically insulated solid electrolyte interface (SEI) that is permeable to lithium ions and impermeable to polysulfide. This not only helps to eliminate side reactions that typically occur between polysulfide anions and lithium metal but also makes it possible to avoid the gradual dissolution of the cathode itself. There are, however other factors at play. The high \(940\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) melting point of commercial lithium sulfide, its low solubility in organic solvents and its high sensitivity to moisture, make it unstable when exposed to atmosphere and thus difficult to infiltrate into conductive carbon hosts using ball milling[24] or through the disperse of lithium sulfide into an anhydrous ethanol solution.[26] Apart from these issues, the high electronic resistivity and poor ionic conductivity of lithium sulfide \((\approx\!10^{-13}\;\mathrm{S\;cm}^{-1})\) make the cathode electrochemically inactive. Cui and co-workers observed that when micrometer particles were used, a large potential barrier \((\approx\!1\;\mathrm{V})\) needed to be overcome before it could be used.[27] In 2014, Cui's research group demonstrated that the encapsulation of lithium sulfide with titanium disulfide as
\(\mathrm{TiS}_{2}\) exhibited high conductivity and high affinity to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{n}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) species.[22] The preparation process, however was quite complicated and involved annealing at a high temperature. To enhance the lithium storage kinetics, a combination of lithium sulfide with electronic conductive materials would be highly desirable for the preparation of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\)-based cathode. For example, Amine's group developed a new approach by burning lithium foils in \(\mathrm{CS}_{2}\) vapor.[25] The structures formed featured crystalline lithium sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)\) particles wrapped in a few layers of graphene and the electrode exhibited a highly reversible capacity of \(1160\;\mathrm{mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after loading at \(10\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Li2S}}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\); these features overcame one of the major constraints associated with the fabrication of traditional lithium sulfide cathode materials, which involves high activation potential, complex fabrication process and high cost. But their long-term cycling stability remains a
concern considering the nonpolar nature of graphene and its lower affinity for polysulfide species with high polarity. In addition, Yu's group also produced \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) via the carbothermic reduction of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) and also simultaneously introduced heteroatoms into the carbon framework to suppress the polysulfide shuttle effect. The authors designed a binder-free \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}\), P–C electrode by blade-coating \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}/\mathrm{C}/\) phytic acid doped polyaniline hydrogel on to current collector,[28] followed by heat treatment under \(900\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in argon atmosphere. This method guarantees that as-made \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) nanoparticles were uniformly confined in the carbon shell and achieved high content of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) (62 wt%) in the electrode considering no additional conducting carbon or binder was added. In addition, the as-made \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}\), P–C cathode exhibits enhanced ionic conductivity compared with other \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) based cathodes due to the formation of \(\mathrm{Li}_{x}\mathrm{PS}_{y}\). Meanwhile, the
author also confirmed that the \(\mathrm{P}\) doping not only suppresses the shuttle effect through the strong \(\mathrm{P}-\mathrm{S}\) interaction but also helps catalyze the redox reactions of sulfur species. This wok threw a promising light for the reasonable design of high content of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) cathode that combines the advantage of improved electronic conductivity as well as strong affinity to polysulfide. Recently, Manthiram's research group proposed an approach to the formation of a stable, conductive encapsulation layer that have a strong affinity to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{n}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) species. Through an in situ surface chemical reaction between the lithium sulfide and the electrolyte that contained additives of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}\),[23] the stable and conductive encapsulation layer containing manganese sulfide (MnS) was formed onto the surface of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) particles during battery operation. Compared with most additives that generate encapsulation layers in the open-circuit configuration, this conductive encapsulation layer is more stable during battery cycling due to the consisting of MnS via a chemical reaction with \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), and the MnS compound is chemically inactive within the cycling window. This works open up new prospects for tuning the properties of electrode materials by selective modifications of the surface electronic and chemical structures desired for \(\mathrm{Li}-\mathrm{S}\) batteries.
Another type of \(\mathrm{Li}-\mathrm{S}\) battery reported was based upon the use of lithium polysulfide cathodes. Instead of using solid sulfur as the active starting material and treated it with a melt diffusion or a liquid infiltration procedure, dissolved lithium polysulfides were dispersed into the host materials uniformly and were thus more accessible by lithium ions and electrons in the electrolyte. This resulted in the improved sulfur utilization and better kinetics compared to reactions that used solid phases as insulating material, and this example is shown in Figure 3b.[12,29,30] However, although the use of soluble polysulfide cathode materials can achieve a high reaction activity, it can also decrease the volumetric energy density of the lithium polysulfide batteries, since an increase in the mass of the electrolyte contributes to the mass of the cell that is inactive.[15] In addition, there are practical problems associated with the synthesis and assembly of this type of battery; most of these are related to the extraordinarily hydroscopic nature of polysulfides. In recent reports, modifications were made to surfaces to address polysulfide deposition problems. For example, at Manthiram's research group,[30] a lightweight 3D nitrogen sulfur co-doped graphene that acted as a current collector was used as an additive binder-free electrode structure and was
shown to accommodate large amount of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) catholyte. With areal sulfur loading of \(8.5~\mathrm{mg_{sul}}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), the capacity stabilized at around \(670~\mathrm{mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g_{sul}}^{-1}\) after 200 cycles at \(0.5~\mathrm{C}\). Cui's research group prepared carbon nanofibers that had tin-doped indium oxide nanoparticles on its surface to form a hybrid 3D electrode, which used \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) as the catholyte.[29] At an areal sulfur loading of \(4~\mathrm{mg_{sul}}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (corresponding sulfur content of \(57~\mathrm{wt\%}\) in the cathode), a reversible specific capacity of \(1034(589)~\mathrm{mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g_{sul(ele)}}^{-1}\) was maintained after 500 cycles at \(0.2~\mathrm{C}\). Cheng and co-workers also reported a 3D conductive porous vanadium nitride nanoribbon/graphene composite, which accommodated \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) as the cathode.[12] With the an areal sulfur loading of \(3~\mathrm{mg_{sul}}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (corresponding sulfur content of \(60~\mathrm{wt\%}\) in the cathode), the capacity was retained at \(917(550)~\mathrm{mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g_{sul(ele)}}^{-1}\) after 200 cycles at \(1~\mathrm{C}\). These results, provide evidence for the potential usefulness of a practical high energy lithium polysulfide battery and provide the impetus for its further exploration.
From the general overview of these four types sulfur-based cathodes, instead of only relying on chemical modification on the surface of carbon-based materials, introducing the metal compounds that possess anchoring sites for chemically binding the polysulfides as the cathode hosts is a significant trend for the development of high-performance Li–S batteries.
## 3. MOFs Used as Cathode Materials in Li–S Batteries
MOFs are materials fabricated from the periodic connection of metal ions with organic linkers and thus is tunable (Figure 4); it can be constructed at the molecular level rationally to form a sulfur host with fully connected mobile dielectron redox centers.[31] MOFs have a highly porous framework, making the mass ionic transportation of relevant ions possible. These porous MOFs have abundant chemical active sites, such as Lewis acid-based or functional organic groups, to form an affinity with sulfur and polysulfide molecules.
MIL-100 (MIL: Materialux Institut Lavoisier) is an example of MOFs solid typically used in Li–S batteries. It is formed from inorganic and organic hybrid material composed of trimesic acid ligands and metal(III) octahedral units. It exhibits a zeotype architecture with two types of mesoporous cages. Its high surface area to volume ratio and its significant number of accessible Lewis metal site make it a useful porous material for impregnation with sulfur. The first report of its use in Li–S battery was in 2011 where it was shown to have weak bonding between the polysulfide anions and MIL-100 (Cr) framework,[32] it was also shown to form couplings with two types of mesoporous cages with a size from 25 to \(29\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) through microporous pentagonal windows with a size of \(5\mathrm{~\AA~}\) and hexagonal shaped windows of \(9\mathrm{~\AA~}\). These windows were reported
In reversibly capture and release polysulfides during cycling. This example is shown in Figure 5a. But in this report, the sulfur content \((19\mathrm{\wt\%})\) in the cathode was too low (the corresponding areal sulfur loading and electrolyte/sulfur ratio were not provided), the capacity of MIL-100/Cr)/S@155+50%C electrode was 461(87) mA h \(\mathrm{g_{sul(ele)}}^{-1}\) after 60 cycles (0.1 C). So, it was difficult to further evaluate the contribution of chemical interaction between the polysulfide and MIL-100 (Cr) framework. In another report, the MOF HKUST-1 was showed to play an important role in the slow release of sulfur into the electrolyte due to a strong confinement effect from the construction of suitable pore spacing and open \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) sites.[33] However, due to its low electronic conductivity, which limited the sulfur utilization, coupling with the low areal sulfur loading of \(0.5~\mathrm{mg_{sul}}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (corresponding sulfur content in the cathode is \(16~\mathrm{wt\%}\), electrolyte/sulfur ratio was not available), the capacity of the MOF HKUST-1/S electrode was limited to 500 (80) mA h \(\mathrm{g_{sul(ele)}}^{-1}\) after 170 cycles and it is shown in Figure 5b. In another report by Li's research group, it was proposed that both the aperture window and the size of the particles play important roles in the rational design of MOF hosts in high-performance Li–S batteries.[34] The authors synthesized ZIF-8 to sizes of \(150~\mathrm{nm}\), \(1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\), and \(3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) and then compared ZIF-8 (3.4 Å) to other HKUST-1, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53, and MIL-53 MOFs hosts using an aperture windows of 6.9, 7.5, and \(8.5\mathrm{~\AA~}\) (Figure 5c), respectively. It can be seen that decreasing the particle size of the MOF host is appreciable to achieve a high capacity. Small apertures, associated with functionalities in the open framework that have affinity with the polysulfide anions, which can help achieve a stable cycling. Together these reports characterized as the first stage in the use of MOFs for Li–S batteries, which mainly focused on the physical confinement of polysulfides.
In the second stage, Xiao et al. started to explore the electrochemical reaction between sulfur and MOFs from the
perspective of "Lewis acid–base interaction" concept.[35] As reported, the Lewis acidic metallic center is inclined to coordinate with soluble polysulfide anions (soft Lewis base) as axial ligand and could effectively entrap the soluble polysulfides within the cathode. Based on this mechanism, the author put forward an order to form stable complexes with metallic centers: \(\mathrm{Mn}\) \(\mathrm{(II)}<\mathrm{Fe(II)}<\mathrm{Co(II)}<\mathrm{Ni(II)}<\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) , providing new insight on the choice of MOFs hosts for Li–S batteries. This conclusion can also be confirmed by the cycling performance of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{6}(\mathrm{BTB})_{4}(\mathrm{BP})_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{BTB})_{4}(\mathrm{BP})_{3}\) as hosts for Li–S batteries (Figure 5d). Although Co-based MOF has improved electronic conductivity than Ni-based MOF, it shows inferior cycling performance due to the weaker coordination between center \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) and polysulfides based on the same amount sulfur content in the cathode (areal sulfur loading, electrolyte/sulfur ratio were not provided). After that, Qian and coworkers proposed that the strong interaction between sulfur species and MOFs are closely associated with the number of Lewis acidic sites in the MOFs.[36] Three forms of MOF electrode materials, MOF-525(2H), MOF-525(FeCl), MOF-525(Cu),
can respectively provide zero, one or two Lewis acid sites that can bind to sulfur. These results were consistent with shifts observed in corresponding X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) binding energies. No obvious chemical shift in the S 2p spectra was observed in \(\mathrm{S@MOF-525(2H)}\) compared to pristine sulfur, while a large S 2p chemical shift of about \(0.7~\mathrm{eV}\) to a lower energy was observed in the \(\mathrm{S@MOF-525(FeCl)}\) and the \(\mathrm{S@MOF-525(Cu)}\) . These significant differences indicated that the Lewis acid metal sites in \(\mathrm{S@MOF-525(FeCl)}\) and \(\mathrm{S@MOF-525(Cu)}\) have strong interactions with sulfur. This is illustrated in Figure 5e. Recently, researchers demonstrated that \(\mathrm{MIL-100(V)}\) might be a host material candidate for Li–S battery.[37] It has characteristics different from other members of the MIL-100 solid family. For example, it contains \(\mathrm{V}^{3+}\) , \(\mathrm{V}^{4+}\) in its vanadyl groups and because different valence states of vanadium ions offer different Lewis acid sites, these can form multiple strength chemical interactions with sulfur and lithium polysulfide. In fact, the cycling stability of \(\mathrm{S@MIL-100(V)/rGO}\) (sulfur content in the cathode: \(35\mathrm{wt}\%\) , areal sulfur loading: \(0.9{-}1.0\ \mathrm{mg_{sul}\ c m^{-2}}\) , electrolyte/sulfur ratio was not available)
was observed to be better than that of \(\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525(2\mathrm{H})\) (sulfur content in the cathode: \(36\mathrm{wt}\%\) , areal sulfur loading: \(0.7~\mathrm{mg_{sul}}\) \(\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , electrolyte/sulfur ratio was not available), indicating the potential of MIL-100(V) as host materials in Li–S batteries, and this is shown in Figure 5f.
Recently, Su et al. demonstrated that sodium iron cyanide \((\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}])\),[13] could confine polysulfides using Lewis acid–base bonding derived from \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II}){-}\mathrm{S}\) interactions. This interaction was observed by shifts in bonding energy of Fe 2p spectra and is shown in Figure 6a. A corresponding UV–vis absorption spectra shown in Figure 6b, and an ex situ Raman spectrum provided further evidence that polysulfide anions formed during the discharge process became entrapped in the cathode. The same authors also proposed that the affinity between the \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) framework and the polysulfide species became stronger with an increase in the length of the polysulfide chain because the coordinated Fe in the \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) acted as a soft Lewis acid and was thus more prone to coordinate with a soft Lewis base. If longer chain polysulfides have a stronger Lewis base, it also has a higher energy binding with \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\). The large interstitial sites, at \(5.27\mathrm{~\AA~}\) by \(5.27\mathrm{~\AA~}\) with \(S_{8}\) at \(4.65\mathrm{~\AA~}\) by \(4.72\mathrm{~\AA~}\) in the
sodium framework, allow toleration in the expansion of sulfur to lithium sulfide. The \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) occupied only about \(67\%\) by volume of the large interstitial sites and this implied that this structure could be useful in alleviating increases in its volume as it is reduced. The authors also calculated out that about \(60\%\) of the sulfur was stored within the large interstitial sites of the sodium \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) while about \(23\%\) of the sulfur covered the surface of the nanocubes, and so there was scope for greater sulfur utilization. Then the author performed a PEDOT coating to accelerate electron transport and improve the sulfur utilization, resulting in \(65~\mathrm{wt\%}\) sulfur content in the cathode (areal sulfur loading of \(1.1~\mathrm{mg_{sul}}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , electrolyte/sulfur was not provided) in the \(\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\ @\mathrm{PEDOT}\) electrodes with a capacity of about 1101(715) mA h \(\mathrm{g_{sul(ele)}^{-1}}\) at \(0.1~\mathrm{C}\) after 100 cycles, and this result is shown in Figure 6c.
Up to now, \(\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\ @\mathrm{PEDOT}\) electrode has the highest sulfur content \((65~\mathrm{wt\%})\) and maximum areal sulfur loading \((1.1~\mathrm{mg_{sul}}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) among the literatures reported about MOFs, based cathodes.[13] The electrochemical performance (1101(715) mA h \(\mathrm{g_{sul(ele)}^{-1}}\) at \(0.1~\mathrm{C}\) after 100 cycles) is very comparative with other MOFs cathode in Li–S battery. Except this report, the general level sulfur content for MOFs cathode
is around \(19{-}48\,\mathrm{~wt}\%\), and areal sulfur loading is less than \(1\,\mathrm{mg_{sul}}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), directly restricting the application of MOFs in high energy density Li–S battery.
Although MOFs have the unique advantages of: (i) high surface area, tunable porosity and variable surface functionality;[31] (ii) both the open metal centers and heteroatomic dopant sites of N, S, and P show strong affinity for polysulfide absorption. Because polysulfide binding energy generated by the Lewis acid–base interaction is around \(3.5{-}7.0\,\mathrm{eV}\), which is the highest level among the four typical types of chemical interactions.[7] But we cannot ignore the key limitations of low sulfur content and areal sulfur loading that directly restrict their widely application in Li–S battery.[13] As for reasons, the low electronic conductivity caused by the existence of organic linkers in MOFs, slow down the redox kinetics of absorbed polysulfides, resulting in low utilization of sulfur and poor cycling performance. From this perspective, future researches may focus on improving the electronic conductivity of MOFs by integrating with some conducting materials (conductive carbon-based or polymer-based materials) and the reported \(\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}](\mathrm{PEDOT}\) is a good example. After PEDOT coating,[13] the electronic conductivity and sulfur utilization were improved.
## 4. Metal Sulfides Used as Cathode Materials in Li–S Batteries
Recently, metal sulfides have been applied as cathode materials in Li–S batteries with the development in the synthesis methods of 2D dichalcogenides. These metal sulfides have various of stoichiometric compositions, crystal structures, valence states, and nanocrystalline morphologies, endowing them with higher electrochemical activities and redox chemistry[38] compared with their metal oxide counterparts.[8]
First in 2014, Cui and co-workers demonstrated a 2D layered transition titanium disulfides for the encapsulation of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) cathodes as it had a conductivity 10 times higher than that between \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and carbon-based graphene and strong binding to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{n}\) species.[22] The results of ab initio simulations demonstrated strong binding between the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{TiS}_{2}\) supported by a strong Li–S interaction between Li atoms in \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), the S atoms in metal disulfides and the strong S–S interaction between sulfur atoms in \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and S atoms in titanium disulfides. This \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}@\) \(\mathrm{TiS}_{2}\) cathode achieved a specific capacity of \(503\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}}{}^{-1}\) at a \(4~\mathrm{C}\) rate and an areal capacity of \(3.0\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at a high mass loading of \(5.3\,\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). Then the author extended this to other layered transition metal disulfides \(\mathrm{ZrS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{VS}_{2}\), and evidenced that the binding energy between the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and these transition metal disulfides \(\mathrm{TiS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{ZrS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{VS}_{2}\) were 2.99, 2.7, and \(2.94\,\mathrm{eV}\), respectively. These values are 10 times higher than those between \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and single layer of carbon-based graphene, which were calculated to be \(0.29\,\mathrm{eV}\). These results opened up the new concept of using 2D layered transition metal disulfides as a general class of effective encapsulation materials for achieving high performance Li–S batteries.
In 2016, Cui and co-workers further promoted their pioneer work on Li–S batteries, discovering that some metal sulfides \((\mathrm{VS}_{2},\ \mathrm{CoS}_{2},\ \mathrm{TiS}_{2})\) that used to be designed for polysulfide binding and trapping can also play an important role as an activation catalyst to promote the oxidation of lithium sulfide \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})\)
back to sulfur (S) during battery charging.[39] The authors using theoretical calculations and experimental design confirmed that the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) decomposition energy barrier is closely related to the binding energy between isolated Li ions and the sulfur in sulfides; the high binding energy between metal sulfides and lithium polysulfides/sulfides always leads to the low \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) decomposition overpotential and energy barrier at the initial charging. Furthermore, the low barrier can facilitate the lithium ion diffusion, promoting the sulfur conversion at the metal sulfide interface and thus improving the performance of Li–S battery. As shown in the Figure 7a, the binding energies between \(\mathrm{VS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) are much higher than that of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{FeS}\), these values are consistent with the lower overpotential and lower energy barrier results shown in Figure 7b,c, the cycling performance in Figure 7d further confirmed that metal sulfides like \(\mathrm{VS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{TiS}_{2}\) with catalyst property for promoting the oxidation of lithium sulfide \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})\) back to sulfur (S) can effectively enhance the performance of Li–S battery.
In another report as shown in Figure 8, Zheng and coworkers innovatively applied in situ transmission electron microscopy (TEM) to observe the sulfur lithiation and delithiation process in \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\)-encapsulated hollow sulfur nanospheres;[40] and confirmed that a \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) encapsulation layer can effectively limit sulfur expansion to less than \(50\%\), a result smaller than the theoretical value of \(80\%\). The authors demonstrated that the reversible lithiation and delithiation of sulfur using a conductive \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) coating that was flexible and had strong van der Waal forces. It confirmed that hermetical encapsulated sulfur particles with \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) cages were effective in preventing soluble polysulfide dissolution by physical confinement and chemisorption. With a sulfur loading of \(1.5\,\mathrm{mg_{sul}}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (corresponding sulfur content of \(65\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) in the cathode), the composite generated a highly reversible specific capacity of \(585(304)\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{sul}(\mathrm{ele})}{}^{-1}\) after 1000 cycles at a rate of \(1\,\mathrm{C}\).
Lee's group reported that the \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2-x}/\) reduced graphene oxide \((\mathrm{MoS}_{2-x}/\mathrm{rGO})^{[41]}\) can accelerate the conversion reactions of polysulfide to insoluble products when applied as cathode host for Li–S battery. As shown in Figure 9. This paper demonstrated that the sulfur deficiencies in the \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) nanoflakes were the catalytic centers that shown good electrochemical activity for \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) deposition. The catalytic effect of \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2-x}\) on the polysulfide redox reaction was confirmed with a cyclic voltammetry (CV) of the symmetric cells run in a \(0.2\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) electrolyte, and is shown in Figure 9a. The \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2-x}/\) rGO with sulfur deficiencies exhibited sharp peak and narrow peak separation for each redox pair of \(-0.39\,\mathrm{V}/0.047\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(-0.047\,\mathrm{V}/0.39\,\mathrm{V}\). This indicated better electrochemical reversibility as well as a facile polysulfide conversion compared to \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/\mathrm{rGO}\) and rGO electrodes. The XPS of the counter electrode of the symmetric cells after scanning from 0 to \(-0.14\,\mathrm{V}\) and then from \(-0.14\) to \(0\,\mathrm{V}\) also proved that sulfur deficiencies lead to the enhanced catalytic activity of the polysulfide electrochemical reaction, and this is shown in Figure 9b. The sulfur-deficient \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) composite shown by a blue curve in this figure, the \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2-x}/\mathrm{rGO}\) had less intensity when scanned to \(-1.4\,\mathrm{V}\) while this sulfur deficient was restored once scanning returned to \(0\,\mathrm{V}\). These observations established the relationship between sulfur deficiencies and the extent and reversibility of the polysulfide conversion. The electrodes
exhibited a high rate capacity of 826.5 (496) mA h \(\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{sul}(\mathrm{ele})}^{-1}\) at an 8 C rate and a good cycle stability of 628.2 (377) mA h \(\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{sul}(\mathrm{ele})}^{-1}\) after 600 cycles at a \(0.5~\mathrm{C}\) rate, making them one of the better polysulfide conversion catalysts reported and one of the better electrodes, as the sulfur content in the electrode was high about \(60\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) and areal sulfur loading was \(1.5\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{sul}}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (electrolyte/sulfur ratio of \(27\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\,\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\)). This last aspect bodes well for the future development of Li–S battery.
Cobalt sulfide binary systems consist of five intermediate phases \((\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3\pm x}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{S}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\)) and these are only \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3\pm x}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{S}\) stable at high temperatures. In addition, \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) \((6.7\times10^{5}\;\mathrm{S}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) \((3.3\times10^{5}\;\mathrm{S}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1})\) exhibit higher conductivity than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) \((1.36\;\mathrm{S}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1})\).[44] Early work by Nazar's group proposed that the high surface area of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) with its hierarchical porosity can provide superior polysulfide absorptivity for long-life and high-loading Li–S batteries.[42]
The heteropolar feature of conductive surface was observed to favor charge transfer from the host material to the polysulfide; the mechanism how polar surface accelerates polysulfide redox reaction was not fully known in this work. Zhang's group reported making a pyrite-type \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\), with a high conductivity to propel the polysulfides redox in Li–S battery.[43] They demonstrated that \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\)-polysulfide interactions not only statically exist but also dynamically accelerate the electrochemical reactions of lithium polysulfide. \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) acts as an effective catalyst tuning the redox reaction of the polysulfide. In a similar report, Pu et al. showed \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) nanotubes have two to three times electrocatalytic capability than \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) in oxygen reduction reactions.[44] The authors confirmed an enhancement in the kinetics of the lithiation/delithiation reaction of the polysulfide when using the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) nanotubes, this catalytic property of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) nanotubes is proved to be very ideal for improving the performance of Li–S batteries. More recently, Jin's group reported an interconnected carbon nanotubes inserted/wired hollow \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) nanoboxes \((\mathrm{CNTs}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\mathrm{-NBs})\) as an efficient sulfur host material.[45] As shown in Figure 10, this hybrid nanostructural host have large void space and a closed structure for accommodating sulfur species and buffering volumetric expansion, which can suppress the polysulfide shuttle effect via physical confinement and chemical interaction; the interlaced CNTs inserted/ threaded into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\)-NBs also provide an integrative conductive network, that facilitates the electron transport all-over the electrode; the high surface area of \(134\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and a total pore volume of \(1.3\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) also enlarge interaction interfaces between CNTs/\(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\)-NBs and polysulfide. Based on this condition, even at an elevated temperature \((50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) with the sulfur content of \(56\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) in the cathode and an areal sulfur loading of \(1.2\,\mathrm{mg_{sul}\,c m}^{-2}\) (electrolyte/sulfur ratio of \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\,\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\)), the
\(\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{CNTs}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\)-NBs cathode shows very competitive cycling stability and excellent rate capability, delivering an initial discharge capacity of 953 (533) \(\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{sul}(\mathrm{ele})}^{-1}\) at \(0.2\,\mathrm{C}\) and the discharge capacity was stabilized at \(781(437)\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{sul}(\mathrm{ele})}^{-1}\) after 300 cycles.
Qiao's group designed a 3D carbon hollow sphere where nanosized NiS could be uniformly distributed, enabling a high areal sulfur loading of \(2.3\,\mathrm{mg_{sul}\,c m}^{-2}\) and sulfur content of \(50\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) in the cathode (electrolyte/sulfur ratio of \(20\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\,\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\)), this \(\mathrm{NiS}@\mathrm{C}\)-HS electrode had a capacity retention of \(96\%\) after 300 cycles at a \(0.5\,\mathrm{C}\).[46] Besides, there have been attempts to apply other metal sulfides in Li–S batteries. Zhang and Tran investigated the use of pyrite \(\mathrm{FeS}_{2}\) and demonstrated the efficient adsorbent of lithium polysulfide in Li–S battery.[47] They also confirmed that the interaction of \(\mathrm{FeS}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{x}\) was due to S–S covalent bonding. Elsewhere, \(\gamma\mathrm{-MnS}\) was tested in Li–S batteries because it is highly active at the surface of porous carbon.[48] \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) was reported having superior electrocatalytic activity in Li–S batteries.[49]
Overall, the general sulfur content in the metal sulfides based cathodes reported up to now is around \(50{-}60\,\mathrm{wt\%}\), the average level of areal sulfur loading are about \(2\,\mathrm{mg_{sul}\,c m}^{-2}\). Among the metal sulfides reported, the \(\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}(15\%)+\mathrm{GO}\) electrode with the high sulfur content of \(60\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) and areal sulfur loading of \(2.9\,\mathrm{mg_{sul}\,c m}^{-2}\),[43] generating the excellent cycling stability within 2000 cycles at \(2\,\mathrm{C}\) rate compared with other sulfides, implying that the catalytic property of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) for tuning the redox reaction of the polysulfide is of great importance for the realization of high-loading and high-rate Li–S battery.
For metal sulfides used as cathode materials for Li–S batteries, they have unique intrinsic benefit in the strong sulfighilic property to sulfur-containing species,[8] therefore, they show
much better polysulfide affinity compared with carbon-based materials. In addition, some metal sulfides like \(\mathrm{VS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{TiS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) can induce lower overpotential compared with commonly used carbon materials,[39] and these metal sulfides also have been confirmed to act as an activation catalyst to propel the polysulfide redox kinetics. On the other hand, many metal sulfides usually have intrinsically poor electrical conductivity, making
the chemically absorbed polysulfide difficult to be directly reduced on the hosts' surface, which greatly limit the sulfur utilization. In addition, some metal sulfides used in Li–S batteries are in the irregular particulate form that can only entrap polysulfide near the surfaces, so when have a high sulfur loading, it is unable to effectively fix all of the polysulfide in the electrode.[50] To maximum synergistic effect of chemisorption and
physisorption, it is better to establish hybrid hollow nanostructure of metal sulfides with 3D conductive carbon-based materials, as this strategy can provide more opportunity to optimize the conductivity, kinetics, and catalytic capability.
## 5. Metal Hydroxides Used as Cathode Material in Li–S Batteries
Considering the high volume ratio of sulfur and metal oxides/ sulfides, it is difficult for the anchoring host materials that with insufficient interface to fix all the polysulfide in the electrode. It was more practical to apply anchoring material as coating layers for the entrapment of polysulfide. Recently, thin layered metal hydroxides, including \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}^{[31]}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\),[52] were reported to encapsulate sulfur and polysulfides in the cathode using their abundant hydrophilic hydroxyl groups. Yu's research group also demonstrated that \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\),[52] could be turned into layered (Li,Ni)-mixed hydroxide compounds by irreversibly reacting with lithium ions during early cycle. This approach was restricted by side effect such as undesirable polarization occurring on the electrodes and the transformation of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\) to layered (Li, Ni)- mixed hydroxides. These reports indicate the usefulness of layered transition metal hydroxides as encapsulation materials for more efficient Li–S batteries.
David Lou's research group demonstrated a novel approach of synthesizing double-shelled nanocages with two shells of cobalt hydroxide and layered double hydroxides \(\left(\mathrm{CH}@\right.\) LDH) as cathode hosts for Li–S batteries and this is shown in Figure 11a.[53] This Ni–Co LDH polyhedral was derived from MOF and preserved the morphology and dimensions of ZIF-67. It provided sufficient space within its void to accommodate a high sulfur content of \(75~\mathrm{wt\%}\) (based on the weight of sulfur in the \(\mathrm{CH}@\mathrm{LDH}/\mathrm{S}\) composite) and strong binding affinities toward polysulfides that induced by its hydroxyl functionalized polar surfaces. This architecture provided sufficient interfaces to fix the polysulfides to the electrode. This idea of introducing layered double hydroxide within Li–S battery is a new approach to the development of high-performance Li–S batteries.
Inspired by this approach, Xu's group developed uniform hollow sulfur nanospheres decorated with ultrathin \(\alpha{\mathrm{-Ni}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanosheets, and this is shown in Figure 11b.[54] This structural design contributes to sufficiently fix polysulfide in the cathode while maintains a high sulfur content. The well-designed hollow nanospheres possess void space to alleviate the volume expansion of sulfur during lithiation. Meanwhile, the sulfur spheres with a diameter of \({\approx}500\ \mathrm{nm}\) and the ultrathin sulfur with a thickness of \({\approx}30\,\mathrm{\nm}\) are favorable for electrons and lithium ion transport, which increase the effective utilization of sulfur. Furthermore, the decorated polar \(\alpha{\mathrm{-Ni}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanosheets on the hollow sulfur nanospheres can chemically
restrict polysulfide dissolution by forming strong chemical bonds and physically confine polysulfide into the spherical structure. Based on this condition, a high sulfur content (81 wt%) was accommodated in the \(\mathsf{S@\alpha-Ni(OH)_{2}}\) composite. In addition, this report also indicated that the metal ions at different valence states on Lewis acid sites formed strong chemical interactions with sulfur and lithium polysulfides, especially when these are at a lower valence state. As seen in the XPS spectrum, Ni, \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) ions were detected in the \(\mathsf{S@N i(O H)_{2}}\) and the variation in the chemical valence of the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) ions suggested strong binding interaction between the sulfur and \(\alpha\mathrm{-Ni(OH)}_{2}\). Therefore, with a high areal sulfur loading of \(2.2\ \mathrm{mg_{sul}\ c m^{-2}}\) and sulfur content of \(56\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) in the
cathode (electrolyte/sulfur ratio was not available), this cathode can generate excellent cycling stability (a capacity decay rate of \(0.04\%\) per cycle) over 1000 cycles at \(0.2\ \mathrm{C}\).
The general level of sulfur content in the metal hydroxides based cathodes is around \(42{-}63\ \mathrm{wt\%}\), and the areal sulfur loading is at the range of \(1.8{-}3\ \mathrm{mg_{sul}\ c m^{-2}}\). According to the literatures reported, with nearly the same amount of sulfur loading, the performance of Ni-based hydroxide greatly exceeds that of Co-based hydroxide, this may be related with the under reported chemical interaction between Ni-based hydroxide and polysulfide is stronger than that of Co-based hydroxide.
Overall, metal hydroxides own various functional polar/ hydrophilic groups (e.g., hydrophilic groups, surface hydroxyl
groups, and so on),[52] combining the low cost and easy preparation characteristics make them a promising class of encapsulation materials for Li–S batteries.[54] However, the low electronic conductivity limits the effective lithium ion transport and sulfur utilization. To improve performance, some metal particles are encouraged to be inserted into the surface of metal hydroxides. In addition, the full anchoring of metal hydroxides to the surface of the sulfur particles has not effectively being realized yet, exploration of metal salts, surfactants and their reaction timing could better optimize the hydroxyl polarity on the surface of the metal hydroxides. Meanwhile, the mechanisms in which the metal hydroxides bind with polysulfide should be further investigated.
## 6. Metal Nitrides Used as Cathode Materials in Li–S Batteries
Metal oxides and hydroxides are typically not conductive and ultimately impede electron transport pathways, leading to low sulfur utilization and rate capability. Therefore, it is of great importance to explore other conductive substrate for Li–S batteries. Transition metal nitrides often crystallize into rock salt structures and display mixed metallic ionic and covalent bonding. Depending on the bonding present, metal nitrides XN, where \( \mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Ti} \), V, Cr, Zn, and Nb, can have high hardness, melting point, and metallic conductivity.[55] These characteristics endow them with a robustness structure that can withstand volume expansion of sulfur during lithiation. Currently, the development of metal nitrides in Li–S battery is in the early stage.
Titanium nitride (TiN) has a high electronic conductivity \((46\ \mathrm{S\ cm^{-1}})\) and excellent chemical stability that are desired for Li–S battery.[62] Goodenough's research group reported the use of a mesoporous TiN as cathode host for lithium sulfur battery.[56] It was confirmed that a strong interaction between the TiN and the polysulfides derived from the N–S surface bonding as it helped to trap soluble polysulfide intermediates within its agglomerations. In addition, the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) passivation layer on the surface of the TiN has hydrophilic groups that provided a polar surface for strong binding with a polysulfide. With sulfur content of \(51\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) and areal sulfur loading of \(1\ \mathrm{mg\ cm^{-2}}\) (electrolyte/sulfur ratio was not available), this cathode kept a capacity retention of \(65\%\) after 500 cycles at \(0.5\ \mathrm{C}\), the corresponding retained capacity was 644 (328) mA h \(\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{sul(ele)}^{-1}}\), meanwhile it also confirmed that both the composition and structure integrity of TiN/S electrode was kept after 500 cycles.
Vanadium nitride (VN) has a number of properties that are useful in sulfur host materials including: (1) Strong chemical absorption of polysulfides thus can suppress the shuttle effect of polysulfides; (2) A high electrical conductivity at \(1.17\times10^{6}\,\mathrm{S\,m^{-1}}\) in room temperature, which is conducive to electrical conversion of absorbed sulfur species on its surface. Li's research group reported a highly conductive porous vanadium nitride nanoribbon/graphene (VN/G) composite that can accommodate large amount of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) catholyte acting as a cathode in Li–S battery, without using carbon black and binder. As shown in Figure 12a,b,[12] this highly conductive 3D interconnected network of VN nanoribbons and reduced graphene oxide (RGO)
sheets possess several micropores and wide range of mesopores \((10{-}50\ \mathrm{nm})\), are able to provide good penetration of electrolyte and hold a large amount of polysulfides, and the skeleton structure not only promotes the electron and lithium ion transportation but also accommodation the volume expansion of sulfur. Moreover, the VN has been proved to have strong chemical absorption to the polysulfide, which can accelerate polysulfide redox reaction kinetics. It was confirmed that the strong absorption of polar VN to the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) was due to the ionic bonding of the V–S and N–Li; these had a bonding energy of 3.75 and \(1.07\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively. The strong polar to polar interaction between VN and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) deforms the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) molecule when it forms three \(\mathrm{S}{-}\mathrm{V}\) and one Li–N bond. The bond lengths of \(\mathrm{S}{-}\mathrm{V}\) at 2.49 to \(2.61\ \mathrm{\AA}\) and Li–N at \(2.08\ \mathrm{\AA}\) are close in bond length to the links formed in bulk VS at \(2.42\ \mathrm{\AA}\) and \(\mathrm{LiNH}_{2}\) at \(2.06\ \mathrm{\AA}\), respectively, and this is shown in Figure 12d. These results confirmed that the high affinity and the strong chemical anchoring of the polar VN to polysulfides. Moreover, the surface of the VN also contained small amounts of V–N–O and V–O bonding, which exhibited additional affinity to polysulfides. Combining all these advantages, with a high areal sulfur loading of \(3\ \mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{sul}}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (corresponding sulfur content of 60 wt%, electrolyte/sulfur ratio around \(31.25\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\ \mathrm{mg}^{-1}\)), this binder-free freestanding 3D cathode generated a specific capacity of \(1131(679)\ \mathrm{mA\ h\ g_{sul(ele)}^{-1}}\) at \(1\ \mathrm{C}\) and a Coulombic efficiency approaching \(100\%\) with a retained capacity of \(917(550)\ \mathrm{mA\ h\ g_{sul(ele)}^{-1}}\) over 200 cycles as shown in Figure 12c, this work opens a new direction for metal nitrides to be used in high energy density Li–S battery.
In a recent report, Dong's research group demonstrated \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) mesoporous spheres can be an excellent host materials for Li–S battery (Figure 13a).[57] The author demonstrated that cobalt with low valency in \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) has a strong chemical affinity for sulfur in the polysulfides. As shown in Figure 13b, Co \(2\mathrm{p}3/2\) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) can be divided into two characteristic peaks located at 780.3 and \(778.3\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively. The peak located at \(778.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) can be assigned to metallic cobalt, illustrating the existence of cobalt with 0 valency, and the peak at \(780.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) assigned to the Co–N bond. After adsorbing \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\), the Co \(2\mathrm{p}3/2\) spectrum has an additional peak at \(778.9\ \mathrm{eV}\), indicating the existence of a Co–S bond and the intensity of the former peaks decreased, especially the peak around \(778.5\ \mathrm{eV}\), confirming the strong affinity for polysulfides. More importantly, the author applied the first cycle CV profiles (Sup P/S, Co \(\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{S}\), Co/S electrodes) to further certify that the \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) sphere can catalyze the oxidation of short-chain to long-chain polysulfides and increasing the capacity of the charge process (Figure 13c), this can be proved by the obvious negative shift in oxidation peak compared with Sup P/S, \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{S}\), Co/S electrodes. In addition, the author further confirmed that \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) spheres not only have stronger affinity for polysulfide compared with corresponding metal oxides \((\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4})\) but can also adsorb more polysulfide. As shown in Figure 13d, after \(12\ \mathrm{h}\), all the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) solutions containing \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) spheres became almost colorless, while only the solution with \(30\ \mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) spheres became colorless. Moreover, the author also demonstrated that one electron lone pair of the N atom can also serve as a conductive Lewis base "catalyst" matrix to enhance the adsorption energy of Li in \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{n}\) \((n=4{-}8)\), which facilitates the oxidization of LiPSs, leading to a better charge capacity and an improved Coulombic efficiency. Therefore, even at a high sulfur
content of \(67\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) and areal sulfur loading of \(2.8\ \mathrm{mg_{sul}\ cm^{-2}}\) (the amount of electrolyte was \(40\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\)), the \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}@S\) cathode can generate a high specific discharge capacity of 1259 (844) mA h \(\mathrm{g_{sul(ele)}}^{-1}\) and maintained a good cycle performance over 800 cycles at \(2\ \mathrm{C}\), as shown in Figure 13e. These results are currently the highest value reported about metal nitrides as cathode hosts for Li–S batteries.
Among the three types of metal nitrides \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\), VN/G, TiN) reported, the generally level of sulfur content loading in the electrodes is around \(50{-}67\ \mathrm{wt\%}\), and the areal sulfur loadings were around \(1{-}3\ \mathrm{mg_{sul}\ cm^{-2}}\) as shown in Table 1, from these perspectives, metal nitrides are promising in the application of Li–S batteries. Based on this condition, \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) based cathode with the highest sulfur content of \(67\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) and areal sulfur loading of \(2.8\ \mathrm{mg_{sul}\ cm^{-2}}\)[57] generated superior cycling performance at high rate (2 C), which are closely associated with the fact that both the Co atoms and N atoms in \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) contribute to propel polysulfide conversion. Besides, the 3D mesoporous nanosphere architecture also facilitates the lithium ion transport thus promotes the polysulfide redox kinetics.
Metal nitrides combine the advantage of excellent electronic conductivity and strong affinity for polysulfides, meanwhile, the physical properties of the metal nitrides XN \((\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Sc}\), Ti, V, Cr, Zr, Nb) possess a unique combination of high hardness, high melting
point, and metallic conductivity,[58] endowing them with robustness structure to withstand the volume expansion during cycling. But the problems of high cost and complicate synthesis pathway may limit its large-scale application in Li–S battery. Based on this condition, exploring the low cost and easy applied nitrogen source will promote its further application in Li–S battery.
## 7. Metal Carbides Used as Cathode Materials in Li–S Batteries
To overcome the insulating characteristic of MOFs/metal oxides/metal sulfides, and combining the advantage of entrapping LiPSs via Lewis acid–base bonding, the exploration for new cathode materials was also extended to MXene, a type of material that is part of a family of semiconducting metallic early-transition metal carbides or carbonitrides. MXene composites are produced by selectively etching atoms from layered MAX phases \((\mathrm{M}_{n+1}\mathrm{AX}_{n}\), \(\mathrm{A}=\mathrm{IIIA}/\mathrm{IVA}\) elements), and then delaminating the sheets using a polar solvent. These types of composites have the general formula \(\mathrm{M}_{n+1}\mathrm{C}_{n}\mathrm{T}_{x}\), where T represents the surface-terminated functional group of OH, F, O, etc. A completely delaminated MXene phase, \(\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{C}\), possesses metallic conductivity with 2D surface that has ample Lewis acid
Ti sites and hydroxyl groups. Nazar's group first reported that the use of 2D conductive MXene nanosheets as cathode hosts to improve Li–S battery performance since the self-functionalized surface formed on the delaminated HF-etched \(\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{x}\) helps to promote chemisorption of polysulfides with "acidic" Ti sites, and this is shown in Figure 14a.[14] The same group recently promoted the understanding about MXene, innovatively proposed the two steps mechanism about MXene entrapping polysulfides, as illustrated in Figure 14b.[59] They demonstrated that hydroxyl terminal groups on MXene undergo a redox reaction
with the polysulfides and form surface thiosulfate groups, which is followed by a Wackenroder reaction that exposes titanium atoms. The metastable titanium atoms accept electrons from polysulfides in the electrolyte to form \(\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{S}\) bonds via a Lewis acid–base reaction. This mechanism was confirmed through a density functional theory calculation that is shown in Figure 14c. The binding energy on pristine MXene was calculated at \(3.42\;\mathrm{eV}\) and attributed to polar to polar interactions. With the removal of hydroxyl groups, the polysulfide can absorb vacancies and bond in the titanium, distorting the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\)
molecule (Figure 14c,i–ii). Especially when all the hydroxyl groups were removed, the maximum value of binding energy was reached. The variation of binding energies of different polysulfide molecules (Li,S, \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) ) also further confirmed that the binding energy increased with the number of surface hydroxyl groups decreased (Figure 14c,iv); fully exposed MXene had the highest binding energy and showed that hydroxyl groups on the MXene were subject to redox activity with the polysulfides. The authors then integrated CNTs into the MXene layers and achieved a porous electronically conductive network with a stable performance; when the areal sulfur loading was \(1.5\;\mathrm{\mg_{sul}\;\;c m^{-2}}\) , \(\mathrm{S/CNT(10\%)}\mathrm{-Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\) electrode had a capacity retention rate of \(48\%\) over 1200 cycles at \(0.5~\mathrm{C}\) , the cycling performance of the thick electrodes (3.6 and \(5.5~\mathrm{mg_{sul}~c m^{-2}}\) , sulfur content of \(64\%\) in the cathode) at a \(0.2~\mathrm{C}\) rate shows a stable performance within 250 cycles (Figure 14d), it also needs to be mentioned that during this stage a low electrolyte/sulfur ratio of \(7~\upmu\mathrm{L}~\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\) was used to minimize the contribution of the electrolyte. Wang's research group successfully synthesized metal carbide \(@\) mesoporous carbon hybrid architecture using a HF-etched \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{Ti}_{x}@\mathrm{Meso-C}\) and pyrolysis of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}/\) MOF-5 by heating at \(900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{~h~}\) under argon and then performing an HF etching step to remove the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) .[60] The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}@\mathrm{Meso-C}\) framework fabricated has the advantages of MXene and a hybrid porous architecture which are favorable for sulfur accommodation. When used as cathode host at sulfur content of \(58\,\textrm{w t}\%\) and areal sulfur loading of \(2\,\mathrm{\mg_{sul}\,\ c m^{-2}}\) accompanying with electrolyte amount of \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) in each coin cell, it maintained a capacity of \(705(408)\,\mathrm{\mA\h\g_{sul(ele)}^{-1}}\) after 300 cycles at \(0.5~\mathrm{C}\) . These results suggest new directions for enriching the electrochemical properties of MXene.
For metal carbides (MXene) as cathode host materials of Li–S batteries, the general sulfur content in the cathodes reported was around \(56{-}64~\mathrm{wt}\%\) , and the areal sulfur loading was in the range of \(1{-}5.5\;\mathrm{mg_{sul}\;c m^{-2}}\) . According to the reported literatures, with the areal sulfur loading below \(1.5~\mathrm{mg_{sul}~c m^{-2}}\) , the strong polysulfide binding energy induced by the Lewis acid–base interaction combining with the high underlying metallic conductivity greatly contributes to the high performance of MXene.
Because of inherently high underlying metallic conductivity and self-functional surface, MXene were regarded as excellent sulfur hosts. In addition, the highly active 2D surface of MXene can chemically bond intermediate polysulfides by Lewis acid–base interaction,[7] making it to be the most promising material for Li–S battery. The difficulty for MXene used as sulfur host is highly depended on the functional groups designed on the surface of MXene materials.[14,59] Obviously, the key research point is to explore the attached functional groups on the surface of MXene in order to realize the high loading Li–S battery.
## 8. Conclusions and Perspectives on Li–S Batteries
Recent advances in the use of metal organic frameworks, metal sulfides, hydroxides, nitrides, and MXene as cathode host materials in Li–S batteries were reviewed. Elements that affecting the electrochemical performance of the Li–S batteries were covered that including (1) sulfur content in the cathode \((\mathrm{wt}\%)\) ,
areal sulfur loading in the electrode \((\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2})\) , and electrolyte/ sulfur ratio \((\upmu\mathrm{L}\,\mathrm{mg}^{-1})\) ; (2) the electronic conductivity of the host material, (3) the chemisorption of the host to the polysulfide, and its corresponding binding energies, (4) the catalytic properties accelerating the conversion of lithium polysulfides and lithium sulfides, (5) cathode architectures. Many works discussed in this paper (Table 1) show great potentials for applied in Li–S batteries with high sulfur accommodation and cycling stability. Of special note are the different valence states of metal ions in the certain MOFs that can offer various Lewis acid sites and form multiple strength chemical interactions with sulfur and lithium polysulfides, which balance the interactions with polysulfides and thus benefit polysulfide mobility, providing new insights that could guide future research in this area. Some metal sulfides with catalytic properties contribute to mutual polysulfide conversion are also appealing in this respect. To achieve cycling stability and high capacity under high sulfur loading, the electronic conductivity of host materials is as important as the surface chemistry used. MXene and metal nitrides combining the advantages of strong affinity for polysulfides and high electronic conductivity are promising in the development of high-performance Li–S batteries.
However, it is generally accepted that the areal sulfur loading of these batteries should be at least \(7~\mathrm{mg_{sul}~c m^{-2}}\) , but the general level reported cannot be comparable with this.[1,7] Significant effort is needed to better areal sulfur loading in long life cathodes at low electrolyte volumes under high current rates. Establishing ultralight conductive networks using flexible materials may be the answer. Development of new electrolytes for high areal sulfur loading cathodes could alleviate polysulfide dissolution and suppress shuttling effects.[61] One solution is to use a high donor number solvent to solubilize the polysulfide to a maximum extent in a minimum amount of electrolyte. Another is a sulfur redox based flow battery where nonsolvent ionic liquids instead of solutions are used forcing the redox chemistry to take place at the battery's interface. In addition, much attention should be also paid to mediate the redox conversion from \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) as this process negatively affects the extended cycling performance by blocking access to the electrode surface. It is expected that much more progress will be made in the near future and some issues raised in this review will be addressed. Given the similarities in the molecular structure between selenium and sulfur and the electrochemical reaction mechanisms between lithium and sodium, results achieved with Li–S battery research could be extended to other batteries such as Li–S batteries, Na–S batteries, and Na–Se batteries.
